!}

= The Red Army of the Ruhr

The strike is over in Berlin. In the Ruhr it has become a war. Since the first days of the putsch, armed miners and steelworkers have driven the Reichswehr and the Freikorps out of Dortmund, Essen, Bochum and Duisburg. By 20 March the whole coalfield is theirs, from the Rhine to Hamm.

They call themselves the Red Army. There may be fifty thousand of them, with machine guns and artillery taken from the army. Its front runs along the Lippe, and it is besieging the fortress of Wesel. Two central councils claim to lead it: one in Hagen, run by the USPD, and one in Essen, where our comrades and the syndicalists are strong.

[? if kapp_outcome = 3 : The workers' government in Berlin has ordered General von Watter not to cross the Lippe. Watter has not yet said whether he will obey. ?][? if kapp_outcome = 1 : In Münster, General von Watter is gathering the same Freikorps that marched with Kapp, and Berlin is letting him. ?]
